__d(function(a,e,t,r,i,n,s){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2013 History",textbook:"Our Pasts \u2013 I",chapter_number:8,chapter_title:"Ashoka, The Emperor Who Gave Up War",estimated_study_time_minutes:50,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Understand how the Mauryan Empire was founded and identify its key rulers","Describe the extent and administration of the Mauryan Empire","Explain what changed Ashoka \u2014 the Kalinga War and its aftermath","Identify the key messages of Ashoka's Dhamma and how he spread them","Understand what Ashoka's inscriptions (edicts) are and why they are historically important","Appreciate why Ashoka is considered one of India's greatest rulers","Connect Ashokan symbols to modern India \u2014 the national emblem and the Ashoka Chakra","Identify the Karnataka connections to the Mauryan Empire and Ashoka's rule"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Knowledge of the Mahajanapadas and Magadha from Chapter 6","Understanding of Buddhism and ahimsa from Chapter 7","Awareness that Magadha was the most powerful Mahajanapada"],introduction:{why_important:"Ashoka was one of the world's greatest emperors \u2014 not because of the battles he won, but because of the one battle that made him stop fighting. His decision to give up war after the bloody Kalinga conquest, and to rule instead through compassion and dhamma, changed the course of Indian and Asian history. His symbols are literally on India's flag and emblem today.",real_life_relevance:"Every time you look at Indian currency, the national flag, or a government document, you see Ashoka's legacy: the Lion Capital from Sarnath is India's national emblem, and the Ashoka Chakra (wheel) is at the centre of the national flag. Ashoka's story asks a powerful question: what is true greatness \u2014 conquering others or conquering oneself?",karnataka_context:"Karnataka is home to some of the most important Ashokan inscriptions in India. The rock edicts at Siddapura, Jatinga-Rameshwara, and Brahmagiri (all in Chitradurga district) are among Ashoka's minor rock edicts. The Mauryan administrative centre of Suvarnaagiri (identified with Kanakagiri in Koppal district) was the capital of the southern Mauryan province \u2014 making Karnataka directly administered by Ashoka's empire. The Sannati stupa and Ashokan inscription (Gulbarga district) is another major find.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Chapter 6 showed how Magadha became the most powerful Mahajanapada. Chapter 7 explained Buddhist teachings. This chapter shows how those teachings transformed an emperor \u2014 Ashoka adopted Buddhism and used its principles to reshape the governance of his vast empire."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"Chandragupta Maurya",role:"Founder of the Mauryan Empire (~321 BCE); defeated the Nanda dynasty and the Greek satrapies left by Alexander; built the first pan-Indian empire"},{name:"Kautilya (Chanakya)",role:"Chandragupta's chief minister and advisor; wrote the Arthashastra \u2014 a masterwork on statecraft, economics, and military strategy"},{name:"Bindusara",role:"Son of Chandragupta; second Mauryan emperor; expanded the empire southward toward the Deccan"},{name:"Ashoka",role:"Third Mauryan emperor (273\u2013232 BCE); conquered Kalinga; was transformed by the war's destruction; adopted Buddhism; ruled through dhamma; issued famous edicts"},{name:"People of Kalinga",role:"The inhabitants of modern Odisha who resisted Ashoka's conquest; 100,000+ killed, 150,000+ deported in the Kalinga War (~261 BCE)"}],plot:["Around 321 BCE, Chandragupta Maurya \u2014 with the guidance of his minister Kautilya \u2014 overthrew the Nanda dynasty and founded the Mauryan Empire, with its capital at Pataliputra (modern Patna, Bihar)","Kautilya's Arthashastra described a sophisticated system of statecraft \u2014 including administration, taxation, espionage, and military strategy","Chandragupta defeated the Greek general Seleucus Nicator and received territory in Afghanistan and Pakistan in exchange for 500 war elephants","Bindusara (son of Chandragupta) expanded the empire further southward","Ashoka, grandson of Chandragupta, became emperor around 273 BCE and consolidated the empire","Around 261 BCE, Ashoka fought the Kalinga War (modern Odisha) \u2014 the bloodiest battle of his reign: 100,000+ people killed, 150,000+ deported, and countless more died from injuries and disease","Ashoka saw the destruction caused by his victory and was filled with deep remorse and horror \u2014 a transformation described in his own inscriptions","Ashoka embraced Buddhism and dedicated the rest of his reign to ruling through 'dhamma' \u2014 a set of moral principles emphasising compassion, non-violence, respect for all religions, and care for subjects","Ashoka issued edicts (official proclamations) carved on rocks and pillars throughout the empire \u2014 these are among the first written records in India using the Brahmi script","His dhamma included: respect for elders and teachers, generosity to the poor, care for animals, religious tolerance, non-violence, and truthfulness","Ashoka built hospitals for humans AND animals, planted trees along roads, dug wells, and set up rest houses for travellers","He sent missionaries \u2014 including his own son Mahinda and daughter Sanghamitra \u2014 to spread Buddhism to Sri Lanka and other parts of Asia","After Ashoka's death (232 BCE), the Mauryan Empire gradually weakened and collapsed by about 185 BCE"],moral:"True greatness lies not in the power to conquer others, but in the wisdom to conquer one's own anger and greed. Ashoka's transformation shows that even the most powerful person can choose compassion over violence."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"The Mauryan Empire \u2014 Foundation and Administration",simple_explanation:"The Mauryan Empire was India's first great empire \u2014 it covered most of the Indian subcontinent. It had a strong central government, a large army, a spy network, and a system for collecting taxes from across the empire.",detailed_explanation:"The Mauryan Empire was founded by Chandragupta Maurya around 321 BCE after he overthrew the Nanda dynasty with the help of his brilliant minister Kautilya (also known as Chanakya or Vishnugupta). The capital was Pataliputra (modern Patna, Bihar). At its height under Ashoka, the empire extended from Afghanistan and Pakistan in the west, to Bengal in the east, and from the Himalayas in the north to most of South India. Kautilya's Arthashastra describes a highly sophisticated administrative system: the emperor sat at the top; below him were ministers (mantris), provincial governors (usually princes), district officers, and village heads. The state maintained a large standing army, an extensive spy network (guptacharas), and collected taxes from farmers (1/6 of produce), traders, and craftspeople. Roads were built across the empire \u2014 enabling trade, military movement, and communication. The Mauryan state was one of the most complex governance systems of the ancient world.",real_life_example:"Think of how a large country like India is governed today: a central government in Delhi, state governments, district collectors, and village panchayats \u2014 all in a hierarchy. The Mauryan Empire had a surprisingly similar hierarchy 2,300 years ago: emperor \u2192 provincial governors \u2192 district officers \u2192 village heads. The idea of layered administration is ancient!",karnataka_example:"The Mauryan province with its headquarters at Suvarnaagiri (identified with Kanakagiri in Koppal district, Karnataka) administered South India on behalf of the emperor. A prince (kumara) served as the governor. Ashokan rock edicts found at Brahmagiri, Siddapura, and Jatinga-Rameshwara (all in Chitradurga district) are direct evidence of Mauryan administration in Karnataka. The Sannati stupa (Gulbarga district) \u2014 with fragments of an Ashokan pillar \u2014 is another major Mauryan site in Karnataka.",common_mistakes:"Students often think the Mauryan Empire included ALL of India. It did not \u2014 the extreme south (Tamil Nadu, Kerala) remained outside it. Also, students confuse Chandragupta Maurya (founder) with Chandragupta II (Gupta dynasty) \u2014 they are completely different rulers from different periods.",memory_tip:"Mauryan founders = CKB-A: Chandragupta (C) founded it, Kautilya (K) advised it, Bindusara (B) expanded it, Ashoka (A) glorified it. C-K-B-A = 'Clever Kings Build Amazing empires'!"},{concept_name:"The Kalinga War and Ashoka's Transformation",simple_explanation:"Ashoka fought a terrible war against Kalinga (modern Odisha). After winning, he saw the enormous suffering he had caused and felt so terrible that he gave up war forever and decided to rule through kindness and dhamma instead.",detailed_explanation:"Around 261 BCE, Ashoka waged war against Kalinga (modern Odisha) \u2014 the last major independent kingdom not yet under Mauryan control. The war was a military success but a human catastrophe: according to Ashoka's own 13th Rock Edict, 100,000 people were killed, 150,000 were deported (taken from their homes), and many more died from wounds and disease. After the conquest, Ashoka personally toured the battlefield. What he saw \u2014 destroyed cities, weeping families, bodies of the dead \u2014 filled him with deep remorse (paschattapa). He wrote: 'This is a matter of profound sorrow and regret to His Sacred Majesty.' Ashoka embraced Buddhism and made a dramatic decision: he would fight no more wars of conquest. He would govern instead through dhamma \u2014 righteous conduct based on compassion, non-violence, and care for all subjects. This was one of the most remarkable personal and political transformations in world history \u2014 a military emperor becoming a peaceful moral ruler because he was horrified by what war does to human beings.",real_life_example:"Think of how veterans of terrible wars sometimes return home changed people \u2014 they have seen such destruction that they devote their lives to peace activism. Ashoka's transformation after Kalinga is the ancient world's most powerful example of this: a conqueror turned into a peace-maker by the suffering he himself caused. His is a story that still resonates with anyone who has witnessed the true cost of violence.",karnataka_example:"The minor rock edicts at Brahmagiri, Siddapura, and Jatinga-Rameshwara in Chitradurga district, Karnataka, were issued after Ashoka's conversion to Buddhism \u2014 they spread his dhamma message in the southern provinces. These edicts were discovered in the 19th\u201320th centuries and were crucial in deciphering the Brahmi script. The presence of these edicts in Karnataka shows that Ashoka's dhamma campaign reached the southernmost parts of his empire.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes think Ashoka became a Buddhist monk after Kalinga and stopped governing. He did NOT \u2014 he remained the emperor until his death (232 BCE). He continued to govern the empire vigorously, but changed his METHODS from military conquest to dhamma-based administration. He remained a king; he just chose different tools of power.",memory_tip:"Kalinga War \u2192 3 Cs: Conquest (won militarily) \u2192 Carnage (100,000 killed) \u2192 Change (adopted dhamma). Ashoka's journey: C-C-C. The third C (Change) is what makes him unforgettable!"},{concept_name:"Ashoka's Dhamma and His Edicts",simple_explanation:"Ashoka's dhamma was a set of moral rules for good living \u2014 respect everyone, be kind, don't harm others. He wrote these rules on rocks and pillars all over India so that everyone could read them. These writings are called 'edicts'.",detailed_explanation:"Ashoka's dhamma (from the Sanskrit 'dharma', meaning duty/righteousness) was not just personal religion \u2014 it was a state policy for moral governance. Key elements of his dhamma: ahimsa (non-violence, especially toward animals); respect for elders, parents, and teachers; generosity to the poor and helpless; tolerance of all religions and sects; truthfulness in speech; and concern for the welfare of all subjects. To spread his dhamma, Ashoka carved proclamations called edicts on large rocks and stone pillars throughout the empire \u2014 from Afghanistan to Karnataka. These are among India's earliest surviving written records, written in Brahmi script. There are Major Rock Edicts (14 edicts on large rocks), Minor Rock Edicts (shorter versions), and Pillar Edicts (on tall stone pillars). James Prinsep deciphered the Brahmi script in 1837, making these edicts readable after 2,000 years. Ashoka also: built roads and planted shade trees; dug wells and rest houses; established hospitals for humans and animals; sent Buddhist missionaries to Sri Lanka, West Asia, and Southeast Asia. He sent his son Mahinda to Sri Lanka \u2014 beginning Buddhism's spread across South and Southeast Asia.",real_life_example:"Imagine a government today that carved its rules and values not just in law books but on public monuments, town squares, and highway pillars \u2014 so that any literate citizen could read them while passing. Ashoka's rock and pillar edicts were precisely this: public, permanent, and widespread government communication. No previous ruler had done anything like it.",karnataka_example:"The Ashokan minor rock edicts at Brahmagiri, Siddapura, and Jatinga-Rameshwara (Chitradurga district, Karnataka) are remarkable specimens. The Brahmagiri edict says: 'I have been a Buddhist layman for more than two and a half years, but I did not make much progress. Now for more than a year I have been more ardent.' This personal confession from an emperor \u2014 carved in stone in what is now Karnataka \u2014 is an extraordinary historical document. India's national emblem (the four-lion capital from the Sarnath pillar) and the Ashoka Chakra at the centre of the national flag both come directly from Ashokan monuments.",common_mistakes:"Students often think Ashoka's dhamma was the same as Buddhism. It was NOT \u2014 it was a broader moral code drawing on Buddhist principles but applicable to people of ALL religions. Ashoka explicitly said his dhamma was not any one religion but a universal ethics of good conduct. He respected all religious traditions equally.",memory_tip:"DHAMMA = Don't Harm Anyone, Make Merit Always. Or: Ashoka's edicts spread 6 key ideas: ANGATT = Ahimsa, Non-violence, Generosity, Ancestral respect (elders), Truthfulness, Tolerance of all religions."}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Mauryan Empire",meaning:"India's first great empire, founded by Chandragupta Maurya around 321 BCE with capital at Pataliputra (Patna). At its peak under Ashoka, it covered most of the Indian subcontinent."},{term:"Pataliputra",meaning:"Capital city of the Mauryan Empire, located at the confluence of the Ganga and Son rivers in modern Patna, Bihar. It was one of the largest cities in the ancient world."},{term:"Arthashastra",meaning:"A treatise on statecraft, economic policy, and military strategy written by Kautilya (Chanakya), chief minister of Chandragupta Maurya. It describes the administration of the Mauryan state."},{term:"Kautilya / Chanakya",meaning:"The brilliant minister and advisor who helped Chandragupta Maurya found the Mauryan Empire. Author of the Arthashastra. Sometimes called the 'Indian Machiavelli'."},{term:"Kalinga",meaning:"An ancient kingdom in modern Odisha. Ashoka's conquest of Kalinga (~261 BCE) resulted in massive destruction (100,000+ killed) and triggered Ashoka's transformation to dhamma-based rule."},{term:"Dhamma",meaning:"Ashoka's moral code of righteous conduct \u2014 including ahimsa, respect for elders, generosity, religious tolerance, and truthfulness. It was NOT one religion but a universal ethics."},{term:"Edict",meaning:"An official proclamation or order issued by a ruler. Ashoka's edicts were carved on rocks and stone pillars throughout the empire \u2014 they are among India's earliest written records."},{term:"Brahmi script",meaning:"The ancient Indian writing system used in Ashoka's edicts. Deciphered by James Prinsep in 1837. Most modern Indian scripts (Devanagari, Kannada, Tamil, etc.) evolved from Brahmi."},{term:"Mahinda",meaning:"Son of Ashoka who was sent as a Buddhist missionary to Sri Lanka, beginning Buddhism's spread across South and Southeast Asia."},{term:"Suvarnaagiri",meaning:"The Mauryan administrative capital for South India, identified with Kanakagiri in Koppal district, Karnataka. A Mauryan prince (kumara) governed the southern provinces from here."}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Comprehension",question:"Why is the Kalinga War considered a turning point in Ashoka's life and reign?",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Describe the Kalinga War \u2014 around 261 BCE, Ashoka conquered Kalinga (modern Odisha). The war resulted in 100,000+ killed, 150,000+ deported, and countless more dead from wounds and disease.","Step 2: Describe Ashoka's reaction \u2014 Ashoka personally witnessed the battlefield and was filled with deep remorse. He wrote about his sorrow in the 13th Rock Edict \u2014 a rare instance of an emperor publicly expressing guilt over his own military victory.","Step 3: Describe the changes \u2014 after Kalinga, Ashoka: (a) embraced Buddhism; (b) gave up war forever; (c) adopted dhamma (righteous conduct) as his governing principle; (d) issued edicts spreading moral values across the empire.","Step 4: Explain why it was a turning point \u2014 before Kalinga, Ashoka was a traditional military emperor expanding through conquest. After Kalinga, he became a ruler focused on welfare, compassion, and moral governance. The war changed not just his personal beliefs but the entire basis of his imperial rule.","Conclusion: Kalinga was a turning point because it transformed Ashoka from a conqueror to a compassionate ruler \u2014 one of history's most dramatic personal and political transformations."]},{example_number:2,type:"Vocabulary in context",question:"Use the word 'edict' in a sentence that explains its historical importance in Ashoka's reign.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Recall meaning \u2014 an edict is an official proclamation or order issued by a ruler, carved on rocks or pillars.","Step 2: Connect to Ashoka \u2014 Ashoka's edicts were carved in Brahmi script across the empire to spread his dhamma message and are among India's earliest written records.","Step 3: Write sentence: 'Ashoka's edicts \u2014 carved on rock faces and stone pillars from Afghanistan to Karnataka \u2014 were revolutionary not just as royal orders but as historical records: they were the first time an Indian ruler communicated directly with his subjects in writing, and their decipherment by James Prinsep in 1837 opened a window into 2,300 years of ancient Indian history.'"]},{example_number:3,type:"Moral application",question:"What does Ashoka's decision to give up war teach us about the nature of true leadership?",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: State what Ashoka did \u2014 after winning the greatest military victory of his reign (Kalinga), he chose to give up war permanently and govern through dhamma (compassion, non-violence, respect).","Step 2: Identify what this shows \u2014 true leadership is not about maximising power; it is about taking responsibility for the consequences of one's actions. Ashoka was powerful enough to keep conquering \u2014 but wise enough to see that conquest caused unbearable suffering.","Step 3: Apply to modern life \u2014 in student life, a true leader is not the most aggressive debater or the one who 'wins' every argument by force. A true leader listens, acknowledges mistakes, and prioritises the wellbeing of the group over personal glory.","Step 4: Conclusion \u2014 Ashoka's example teaches that greatness is measured not by how many people you defeat, but by how many lives you improve. His dhamma \u2014 still visible in India's national emblem \u2014 is a reminder that the most enduring legacy of a leader is built through compassion, not conquest."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Ashoka's Dhamma and His Edicts",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"Who founded the Mauryan Empire and what was its capital?",a:"Chandragupta Maurya founded the Mauryan Empire around 321 BCE. Its capital was Pataliputra (modern Patna, Bihar)."},{type:"Recall",q:"How many people were killed and deported in the Kalinga War?",a:"About 100,000 people were killed and 150,000 were deported (taken from their homes) in the Kalinga War, according to Ashoka's own 13th Rock Edict."},{type:"Understanding",q:"What is the difference between Ashoka's dhamma and Buddhism?",a:"Ashoka's dhamma was a broader moral code \u2014 not specifically Buddhist \u2014 that applied to people of ALL religions. It included ahimsa, respect, generosity, and tolerance. Buddhism is a specific religion with its own doctrines. Dhamma drew on Buddhist principles but was designed to be a universal ethics for all subjects."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Why are Ashoka's edicts historically important?",a:"Ashoka's edicts are among India's earliest surviving written records; they are written in Brahmi script; they show how Ashoka governed and what he valued; and deciphering them (by James Prinsep, 1837) opened up much of ancient Indian history to modern scholars."},{type:"Application",q:"How is Ashoka's legacy still visible in modern India? Give two examples.",a:"1. India's national emblem \u2014 the four-lion capital from Ashoka's Sarnath pillar. 2. The Ashoka Chakra \u2014 the 24-spoked wheel at the centre of the Indian national flag, taken from Ashokan pillar art."}]}],key_takeaways:["Mauryan Empire founded by Chandragupta Maurya (~321 BCE); capital: Pataliputra (Patna, Bihar).","Kautilya (Chanakya) was Chandragupta's minister; wrote the Arthashastra on statecraft.","Three Mauryan emperors: Chandragupta (founder) \u2192 Bindusara (expander) \u2192 Ashoka (the greatest).","Kalinga War (~261 BCE): 100,000+ killed, 150,000+ deported \u2014 Ashoka's own figures from the 13th Rock Edict.","After Kalinga, Ashoka embraced Buddhism and adopted dhamma \u2014 compassion, ahimsa, tolerance, generosity.","Ashoka's dhamma was NOT just Buddhism \u2014 it was a universal moral code for all subjects of all religions.","Edicts: official proclamations carved on rocks and pillars in Brahmi script \u2014 among India's earliest written records.","James Prinsep deciphered Brahmi script in 1837, making Ashokan edicts readable again.","Ashoka sent son Mahinda and daughter Sanghamitra to Sri Lanka \u2014 spreading Buddhism across Asia.","Karnataka links: Ashokan edicts at Brahmagiri, Siddapura, Jatinga-Rameshwara (Chitradurga); Suvarnaagiri (Koppal); Sannati stupa (Gulbarga); India's national emblem = Ashokan Lion Capital."],mind_map:{root:"Ashoka, The Emperor Who Gave Up War",branches:[{branch:"Mauryan Empire",sub:["Founded by Chandragupta (321 BCE)","Capital: Pataliputra","Kautilya wrote Arthashastra","Bindusara expanded south"]},{branch:"Kalinga War (~261 BCE)",sub:["100,000+ killed","150,000+ deported","Ashoka filled with remorse","Turning point of his reign"]},{branch:"Ashoka's Dhamma",sub:["Ahimsa (non-violence)","Respect for all religions","Care for poor and animals","Universal ethics, not just Buddhism"]},{branch:"Edicts",sub:["Rock edicts (14 major)","Pillar edicts","Brahmi script","Prinsep deciphered in 1837"]},{branch:"Karnataka Connections",sub:["Brahmagiri, Siddapura edicts (Chitradurga)","Suvarnaagiri \u2013 south HQ (Koppal)","Sannati stupa (Gulbarga)","National emblem = Ashokan Lion Capital"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 8 covers the Mauryan Empire and Ashoka. The Mauryan Empire was founded by Chandragupta Maurya (~321 BCE) with capital at Pataliputra (Patna), aided by minister Kautilya (author of the Arthashastra). Three key rulers: Chandragupta (founder), Bindusara (expander), Ashoka (greatest). Ashoka conquered Kalinga (~261 BCE): 100,000+ killed, 150,000+ deported. Horrified by the destruction, Ashoka embraced Buddhism and adopted 'dhamma' \u2014 a moral code of ahimsa, respect, generosity, and religious tolerance \u2014 as the basis of his rule. He issued edicts on rocks and pillars across the empire in Brahmi script \u2014 among India's earliest written records. James Prinsep deciphered Brahmi in 1837. Ashoka built hospitals, roads, wells, and rest houses; sent son Mahinda and daughter Sanghamitra to Sri Lanka to spread Buddhism. Karnataka: Ashokan edicts at Brahmagiri, Siddapura, Jatinga-Rameshwara (Chitradurga district); Suvarnaagiri (Koppal) = Mauryan south capital; Sannati stupa (Gulbarga). India's national emblem = Ashokan Lion Capital (Sarnath); Ashoka Chakra = centre of Indian flag. Key terms: Pataliputra, Arthashastra, Kautilya, Kalinga, dhamma, edict, Brahmi, Mahinda.",must_remember_facts:["Mauryan Empire founded: Chandragupta Maurya, ~321 BCE. Capital: Pataliputra (Patna, Bihar).","Kautilya = Chanakya = Chandragupta's minister; wrote Arthashastra (textbook on statecraft).","Three Mauryan rulers: Chandragupta \u2192 Bindusara \u2192 Ashoka.","Kalinga War: ~261 BCE; 100,000+ killed; 150,000+ deported (Ashoka's own 13th Rock Edict).","Ashoka's dhamma: ahimsa, respect for all religions, generosity, care for animals and humans.","Dhamma \u2260 just Buddhism \u2014 it was a universal moral code for ALL people.","Edicts: carved on rocks (Major Rock Edicts) and pillars; written in Brahmi script.","Brahmi script deciphered by James Prinsep in 1837.","Ashoka sent son Mahinda and daughter Sanghamitra to Sri Lanka to spread Buddhism.","Karnataka: edicts at Brahmagiri, Siddapura, Jatinga-Rameshwara (Chitradurga); Suvarnaagiri (Koppal) = Mauryan south capital; India's national emblem = Ashokan Lion Capital from Sarnath."]}},2548,[]);